#!/usr/bin/perl
# lzrprf: owns the one window of an application, runs the application inside a
# container that this file defines, and does for it the one thing it may have
# done: fetch the https address that you typed into the bar of that window.
#
#   lzrprf run /path/to/program [arguments]     open the window, with the program contained behind it
#   lzrprf incognito [/path/to/program [args]]  the same in an incognito window, which leaves nothing behind when it closes [C16]
#   lzrprf log                                  print what the log on this computer holds
#   lzrprf keep [nothing|hosts|all [DAYS]]      say, or choose, what is kept after a window closes
#   lzrprf address TEXT...                      say what would be fetched if TEXT were typed into the bar
#   lzrprf request TEXT...                      print the exact bytes a site is sent for TEXT
#   lzrprf public NUMBER...                     say whether a numeric address is on the public internet, and how this computer would send to it
#   lzrprf mark                                 this build of macOS, this file and the selftest beside it (each file by its SHA-256), as one line; an empty line when no selftest can be read beside it
#
# The application is treated as hostile. It has no window, no network, no
# file of yours and no service of the system: it is started once for each answer
# lzrprf has already fetched, reads that answer, and writes a list of what to
# draw. lzrprf checks every line of the list and draws it. The window, its
# address bar, its buttons and its words are lzrprf's, made by the text at the
# end of this file.
#
# Three kinds of process are started here, each inside a container:
#   the PAGE container holds the application, once for each answer;
#   the FETCH container holds the system's own curl, once for each request;
#   the WINDOW container holds the window.
# What is outside every container is this file: it looks up the name, makes
# the one connection, writes the log, and checks what crosses.
#
# THE CONDITIONS. Each promise lzrprf makes is a condition in this file, and
# the line that holds it carries its mark. The README says the same in words
# and cites these marks, and selftest fails when a mark is cited that is not
# here, or the other way round.
#
#   [C1]  The application is started in one place only, and only inside the page container.
#   [C2]  At every start lzrprf tries, from inside that same container, what the container must refuse, and starts nothing unless all of it was refused and the same tries worked outside.
#   [C3]  No process is handed a byte of what was fetched, nor the connection to a site, before the system itself says that this process is contained. Before that, of what you asked for each knows the address alone: the application in one variable, curl among its fixed switches.
#   [C4]  A request is made only for an address that came from the window's bar or was typed into its own bookmark modal, at once or kept as the home page or a bookmark, or that a page redirected to and you then said yes to in the window, or for one of three fixed addresses of the release file after you said yes (the newest release number, a report, the installer in its one or two places), or for a picture or a style sheet that the page laid out from such an address names on that same host, at most eight of each, by a path lzrprf checked. There is no fourth way to a request.
#   [C5]  Nothing is looked up or fetched before its line is in the log: in the memory of the process behind the window, and on the disk too when you chose to keep a log. No answer is shown whose line could not be recorded.
#   [C6]  There is one place that connects to a site, and it connects to a numeric address that was checked and written down.
#   [C7]  What a site is sent is made from the address alone, with one fixed browser name, LZRBRWSR: the same request from every computer, byte for byte, with nothing kept from an earlier request.
#   [C8]  A host inside your own network is fetched only after you said yes to exactly that, once.
#   [C9]  Every line the application writes is checked before the window is given it, and what is not one of five kinds of line, or a laid page, is not drawn at all. Its title, one line of at most 200 characters with every control and format character taken out, is shown in its tab and nowhere else.
#   [C10] The window's process has no network of its own, writes no file, starts no other program, opens nothing through the system, commands no other program, sends no signal to another process, and reads nothing under /Users or on another disk but your settings, fonts, keyboard layouts and input methods. Before the window takes an address or shows a fetched page, lzrprf asks the system about eight operations of that process (connecting out, creating a file, starting a second process, reaching a system service when none is named, opening something through the system, commanding another program, writing your settings, sending a signal), and each must be refused; the rest of that container's text, its list of services among it, is not asked about, and selftest makes its own tries from the real window. Of the system's services it looks up by name only the 41 its container lists and one local name, the clipboard's.
#   [C11] No page is fetched or shown before selftest held on this build of macOS with this file and the selftest beside it, in its last run there. The window is there earlier, with its bar switched off: it shows lzrprf's own questions and no page. A check the test could not try there is named in its mark, and then no page is fetched or shown before you said yes to starting without exactly those checks.
#   [C12] Nothing of what you fetched stays on the disk unless you chose a kept log, apart from the one address you made the home page, the bookmarks you made or imported (each a name and an address) and the order of each of their folders (a file .order of their names), whether the bookmarks bar is shown, and an installer you chose to run, with what it printed (install.txt); a kept log holds hosts only unless you chose full addresses. It is only added to, apart from clearing by your button and the days you chose: in a kept log each of the two says so in its first line, and with nothing kept, clearing removes the log whole and the window's lines say so.
#   [C13] The application opens no file for its fonts: lzrprf hands it eight fonts of the Mac, by fixed paths, in front of a page's answer and never a picture's, each a plain file and no link of at most 2 MiB that begins as a font, or with length 0; and only after the system said that the application is contained.
#   [C14] A page is laid out at most twice: a second time only when its first run named style sheets on the page's own host, at most eight, which lzrprf fetches and hands the second run in front of the answer, after the fonts, each at most 1 MiB or with length 0. A second run that names style sheets again, or is not a page, is not shown, the first run's page is; there is never a third run, and a picture run is handed no style sheet.
#   [C16] An incognito window writes nothing that outlives it. Its log, history, tabs, suggestions and everything it fetched are held in its memory or in one folder it makes for itself, readable by you alone, in your own temporary folder (TMPDIR, /private/var/folders/.../T), named lzrprf-i.<its process number>.<12 random hex digits>. Of ~/.lzrprf it reads, and never writes, the self-test's result (proven, untested, selftest.lock), the home page, the search address, whether the bookmarks bar is shown and the bookmarks. The folder is removed when the window ends, however it ends: quit, its last window closed, a signal, its process dying; and a folder left by a run whose processes are all gone is removed at the next start of any lzrprf window.
#   [C15] Another browser's bookmarks are read only after you chose that browser, or a bookmarks file, in the window's import wizard, by lzrprf itself and never by a container or the window, with no program started: Local State and each profile's Bookmarks of Chrome, Edge, Brave and Vivaldi, Firefox's profiles.ini and newest bookmarkbackups/*.jsonlz4, Safari's Bookmarks.plist, all at fixed places under your home folder, or an HTML bookmarks file of yours found in Downloads, Desktop or Documents or typed as a path in your home folder; each a plain file of yours and no link, of at most 32 MiB. Nothing in them is fetched, and nothing of them is written but the bookmarks kept after you pressed Import [C12].
#
# This file is the whole program. Its parts, in order: the containers, the
# proof that they hold, one visit, the fetch, the window's side, the log, the
# window itself.
use strict;
use warnings;
use Cwd 'abs_path';
use Digest::SHA;
use Fcntl qw(:flock O_RDONLY O_RDWR O_WRONLY O_APPEND O_CREAT O_EXCL O_NOFOLLOW O_NONBLOCK);
use File::Basename qw(basename dirname);
use File::Glob 'bsd_glob';
use File::Path 'remove_tree';
use IO::Handle;
use IO::Socket::INET;
use IO::Socket::UNIX;
use MIME::Base64 qw(decode_base64 encode_base64);
use POSIX 'strftime';
use Socket qw(AF_INET AF_INET6 SOCK_STREAM SOL_SOCKET SO_ERROR NI_NUMERICHOST NI_NUMERICSERV AI_NUMERICHOST inet_aton inet_pton pack_sockaddr_in getaddrinfo getnameinfo);
use Time::HiRes;

$^X eq '/usr/bin/perl' or exec '/usr/bin/perl', $0, @ARGV or die "lzrprf: the system's own perl is missing\n";
umask 0077;                               # what lzrprf writes is readable by you alone
{ my $number; do { $number = POSIX::open('/dev/null', O_RDWR) } while defined $number && $number < 3; POSIX::close($number) if defined $number }    # descriptors 0, 1 and 2 exist from here on
%ENV = (PATH => '/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin', map { defined $ENV{$_} ? ($_ => $ENV{$_}) : () } qw(LANG TMPDIR __CF_USER_TEXT_ENCODING));    # nothing lzrprf starts is found through, or told, a variable of whoever started lzrprf

my $SELF = abs_path($0);
my $HOME = abs_path((getpwuid $<)[7]);    # from the account, not from the environment
my $DIR = "$HOME/.lzrprf";                # no container can read or write this folder
my ($INCOGNITO, $BASE) = (0, undef);     # an incognito window [C16]: $DIR is then its own folder in your temporary folder, and $BASE the folder its few settings are read from, never written
my $UNKEPT = 'An incognito window writes nothing';    # the start of every refusal to keep something in one
my ($HELD_ENGINE, $HELD_BAR);             # in an incognito window, a search address or a bookmarks bar chosen in it: in its memory only
sub kept_in { $INCOGNITO ? $BASE : $DIR }    # where settings are read from: ~/.lzrprf (or a test's folder), also in an incognito window
my $CURL = '/usr/bin/curl';               # the system's own; it does the https, lzrprf holds no code for it
my $SCRIPT = '/usr/bin/osascript';        # the system's own; it runs the window's text and the question to the system
my $ROOTS = '/private/etc/ssl/cert.pem';  # the system's list of certificate authorities
my $FETCH_BYTES = 6 * 1024 * 1024;        # the most that is taken from a site for one address
my $FETCH_SECONDS = 30;                   # the most one request may take, from the first connection to the last byte
my $WIRE_BYTES = $FETCH_BYTES + 1024 * 1024;    # the most that is carried from the site to curl: the answer, and what encryption adds
my $SENT_BYTES = 64 * 1024;               # the most that is carried from curl to the site: one request is far smaller
my $FIND_SECONDS = 10;                    # the most a name lookup may take
my $FOUND = 8;                            # the most addresses of one name that are taken: the first eight this computer has a way to
my $LOOKED = 16;                          # the most addresses of one name that are looked at for that: what the lookup gave beyond them is left out
my $PAGE_BYTES = 12 * 1024 * 1024;        # the most one page container may write: room for the 32 tiles of a laid page, 32 x 262,656 bytes of base64, about 8.4 MB, and the rest of the page
my $PAGE_SECONDS = 30;                    # a page container is ended after this, whatever it is doing
my $PAGE_ROOM = 2097152;                 # the address space, in KiB, a page container may take on top of what the shell that starts it holds: 2 GiB. What the application itself holds at its start is not measured
my $PIECES = 50_000;                      # the most lines one page may have
my $BOXES = 2000;                         # the most grey boxes among them
my $BLOCK = 20_000;                       # the most one block of a page may hold, from one line that begins a block to the next: a line of text counts its characters, a line break one, a grey box $BOX
my $BOX = 400;                            # what a grey box counts in a block: more than the widest and flattest one becomes in the window
my $HUNG = 30;                            # the most characters in a row that hang on the one before them
my $WORD = 1000;                          # the most characters in a row with no place among them where a line may end
my $JOINER = qr/(?!\x{103a})\p{Ccc=9}/;    # a sign that joins the letter after it to the one before (Myanmar's asat ends a syllable and joins nothing)
my $HANGS = qr/[\p{M}\p{GCB=Extend}\p{GCB=SpacingMark}\p{GCB=Prepend}\p{GCB=V}\p{GCB=T}]|(?<=\p{GCB=L})\p{GCB=L}|(?<=\p{GCB=Prepend})[^\x{fffd}]|(?<=$JOINER)[^\x{fffd}]|(?<=$JOINER\p{M})[^\x{fffd}]/;    # a character that hangs on the one before it: see "What the window is spared"
my $SOLID = qr/[^\x{20}\x{1680}\x{2000}-\x{2006}\x{2008}-\x{200a}\x{205f}\x{3000}\x{200b}]/;    # a character where no line may end: all but the spaces that let one end (a no-break space does not)
my $MADE = 120;                           # the most lines that say what the page is made of
my %LAID = (R => 20_000, X => 30_000, G => 1000, M => 64, I => 1000, K => 32, J => 1024);    # a laid page: the most rectangles, runs of text, grey boxes, pictures, places for a fetched picture, tiles (numbered under K) and places of a tile
my $RUN_TEXT = 2000;                      # the most characters in one run of text of a laid page
my $ALL_TEXT = 1_000_000;                 # the most characters in all its runs together
my $PIXELS = 262_144;                     # the most pixels in all its pictures together: one picture of 512 by 512
my $PICTURES = 8;                         # the most pictures one page may name on its own host, each fetched by a run of its own
my $PICTURE_SECONDS = 20;                 # one clock for all of a page's pictures: a picture not begun by then stays a grey box
my $PICTURE_PIXELS = 200_000;             # the most pixels in one fetched picture
my $SHEETS = 8;                           # the most style sheets one page may name on its own host, fetched before it is laid out a second time
my $SHEET_BYTES = 1024 * 1024;            # the most of one style sheet's answer that is handed over: a larger one goes with length 0
my $SHEET_SECONDS = 10;                   # one clock for starting all of a page's style sheets: a sheet not begun by then goes with length 0
my $DRAWN_PIXELS = 8 * $PICTURE_PIXELS;   # the most pixels the window is given for fetched pictures on one page, however often one is placed: a place beyond that stays a grey box
my @FACES = map { "/System/Library/Fonts/Supplemental/$_.ttf" } 'Arial', 'Arial Bold', 'Arial Italic', 'Arial Bold Italic', 'Courier New', 'Courier New Bold', 'Courier New Italic', 'Courier New Bold Italic';    # the fonts a page is drawn with, in the application's order: plain, bold, italic, both, then the same in fixed width
my $FACE_BYTES = 2 * 1024 * 1024;         # the most of one font that is handed over: the largest of the eight is 773,236 bytes on this macOS
my $ANSWER_SECONDS = 600;                 # a question that is still open then counts as answered with its first button
my $NAME = qr/(?:[A-Za-z0-9-]{1,63}\.)+[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9-]{0,62}/;    # a host name: never a bare number, never one word
my $UNWRITTEN = 'Nothing was fetched: the log could not be written.';
my $UNENDED = 'lzrprf tried the fetch. Nothing of its answer is shown: the line that says how it ended could not be written to the log.';    # not the words above: by then the site was connected to, or was tried
my $UNSAID = 0;                           # set while a fixed request (sub fixed) was tried and the line of how it ended is not in the log yet
my $KEPT_DAYS = 30;                       # how long a kept log keeps a line, when you chose a kept log and no number of days
my $KEPT_MOST = 3650;                     # a setting of more days than this is no setting: nothing is kept

# "lzrprf try" is the same program for tests (selftest, and nothing else):
# its state is in a folder of its own, every name leads to one listener on
# this computer, whose certificate must be signed by the test's own
# authority, and with "allow" or "deny" there is no window: what the window
# would be told is printed, what would be typed is the test's text, and every
# question gets that one answer; with a row of the digits 1 and 2 instead,
# each question in turn gets the next one, and once the row is used up every
# question is answered as nobody answering would. With "ask" the real window runs, and the
# test may add text of its own to the window's. A test that puts a file
# "prove" into its folder gets the question of [C11] as well, against a mark
# in that folder. "lzrprf run" has none of this.
my ($TRY, $PEER, $TYPED, $ANSWER, $RUN, $APP, $PROGRAM, @ARGUMENTS) = (0, '-', '-', 'ask', 0, 'the application', '');
my $BEGAN;                                # the SHA-256 of the program when this window began: see "sub laid_out"
my ($FROM, @EVENTS);                      # what the window says; or, without a window, what a test has typed
my $DRAWN = 0;                            # a page was handed to the window, and the window has not said yet that it could not draw it
my @VIEW_WIDE = (320, 1600);              # the narrowest and the widest a page is laid out: a window's width outside them is taken as the nearer one, and the application refuses a =view frame outside them
my @VIEW_HIGH = (240, 2160);              # the same for the height of its first screen, which a page's vh is taken from
my @VIEW = (960, 768);                    # the size of the window's page, in points, as the window last said it and held to those bounds; until it says one, the size every page was laid at before
my $LAID_WIDE;                            # the width the run being checked was asked to lay its page at: a laid page of any other width is unfit
my $SHOWN;                                # the page shown, kept to lay it out again when the window's page changes width: its address, its answer, the frame of style sheets it was laid with, whether that was a second run, its host, its decoded pictures, the width it was laid at
my @HELD;                                 # what the window said while lzrprf looked for its latest size, in order: heard before anything new
my $TABS_MOST = 32;                       # the most tabs one window holds: the owner's order of 2026-11-20
my $TITLE = 200;                          # the most characters of a page's title, the =title line, shown in its tab
my $VISITED = 100;                        # the most addresses a tab's history holds, the oldest dropped first: a list nothing is fetched from, emptied by "Clear history"
my $INSTALL_SECONDS = 1800;               # the installer of a newer release is ended after this long: the slowest install measured by hand took under two minutes
my $INSTALL_TRIES = 12;                 # how often the installer is fetched from both places while the two differ: a release being published reaches one place minutes before the other
my $INSTALL_WAIT = 30;                   # the seconds between those tries: twelve cover the five minutes one place may keep its old copy
my $INSTALL_LINE = 2000;                  # the most characters of one line the installer printed that the window is given; a longer one is given in parts
sub fresh_tab { return { url => '', title => '', visited => [], at => -1, shown => undef, says => undef, secure => undef } }    # a tab: the address in its bar, its page's title, the addresses it went to and where in them it stands (back and forward), its page shown as $SHOWN keeps it, lzrprf's last words in it, what lzrprf saw of the connection its page came over
my @TABS = (fresh_tab());                 # the window's tabs, in the order the window shows them; the page of the selected one is the only one shown, and it is $SHOWN while it is selected
my $TAB = 0;                              # which tab is selected
my $NEXT;                                 # set by sub visit when you chose to follow a redirect: [its address, how its log line begins, undef] (sub go)
my @WAY;                                  # the redirects of one go, each "FROM answered STATUS, to TO (how)": what the lock tells of how the page shown was reached (sub go, sub secured)
my %ENGINES = (brave => 'https://search.brave.com/search?q=%s', duckduckgo => 'https://html.duckduckgo.com/html/?q=%s', google => 'https://www.google.com/search?q=%s');    # the search addresses offered by name (sub typed)
my $WROTE = "\nlzrprf-certificates\n";    # the line curl writes after the whole answer, before the certificates it checked (sub fetch)
my @MARKS;                                # the bookmarks as last told to the window, by number: [its file, its name, its address, its folder] (sub marks_told)
my $TITLED = '';                          # the title of the page the last checked run wrote, '' for none (sub checked)
my %MORE = (nothing => 0, hosts => 1, all => 2);    # what is kept, from less to more
my $HALF;                                 # the line of a change reached the log, and the system did not say that it is on the disk
my (@SESSION, $UNMARKED, $MARKED, $UNEXPIRED);    # this window's lines, in the memory of the process behind it and nowhere else; whether a log could not be marked for Time Machine; whether this process marked it; whether the days you chose still wait for another window's request to end

sub now { Time::HiRes::clock_gettime(Time::HiRes::CLOCK_MONOTONIC()) }    # a clock nobody can set: seconds since the computer started

# What the operating system allows inside the page container and the fetch
# container. Every line is an allowance; what is not listed is refused: every
# network use, writing any file, reading any file outside the system's own
# folders and the application's own folder, starting any other program, starting a second process, signalling
# another process, asking another program to open something, every system
# service (so: no window, no key, no clipboard, no other application), and
# every fact about the computer that is not under the three prefixes or among
# the single names below (so: not its name, not its other processes, not its
# network). As code it may load itself and the system's own libraries; from
# release 17 on no other file, also not one in its own folder. That takes a
# refusal of its own: macOS lets a contained program load as code whatever
# file it may read, unless a line says no, so before release 17 the line that
# allows was all there was and it refused nothing.
# One thing macOS does not refuse by itself inside a container: making (not
# using) memory, flags and queues of the old "System V" kind, of which the
# whole computer has only a few. The three calls that make them are refused
# by name, so a contained program cannot use them up for other programs.
my $CONTAINER = <<'END';
(version 1)
(deny default)
(deny syscall-unix (syscall-number SYS_shmget SYS_semget SYS_msgget))
(allow process-exec (literal (param "APP")))
(deny file-map-executable)
(allow file-map-executable (subpath "/System") (subpath "/usr/lib") (literal (param "APP")))
(allow sysctl-read
  (sysctl-name-prefix "hw.") (sysctl-name-prefix "machdep.cpu.") (sysctl-name-prefix "kern.osvariant")
  (sysctl-name "kern.ostype") (sysctl-name "kern.osrelease") (sysctl-name "kern.osversion")
  (sysctl-name "kern.osproductversion") (sysctl-name "kern.osproductversioncompat") (sysctl-name "kern.version")
  (sysctl-name "kern.maxfilesperproc") (sysctl-name "kern.argmax") (sysctl-name "kern.ngroups")
  (sysctl-name "kern.usrstack64") (sysctl-name "kern.secure_kernel") (sysctl-name "kern.willshutdown")
  (sysctl-name "kern.hv_vmm_present") (sysctl-name "vm.footprint_suspend")
  (sysctl-name "sysctl.proc_native") (sysctl-name "sysctl.proc_cputype") (sysctl-name "sysctl.proc_translated"))
(allow file-read*
  (subpath "/System") (subpath "/usr/lib") (subpath "/usr/share") (subpath "/private/etc/ssl")
  (literal "/") (literal "/dev/null") (literal "/dev/random") (literal "/dev/urandom")
  (subpath (param "OWN")))
(deny file-read* (subpath "/System/Volumes"))
(allow file-read* (subpath "/System/Volumes/Preboot/Cryptexes"))
(allow file-read-metadata
  (literal "/") (literal "/usr") (literal "/Library") (literal "/dev") (literal "/private")
  (literal "/private/etc") (literal "/private/var") (literal "/private/var/db") (literal "/etc") (literal "/var")
  (literal "/System/Volumes") (literal "/System/Volumes/Preboot")
END

# The page container is allowed nothing on top of that. It also gets at most
# 30 seconds of processor time, may write no byte into a file and no memory dump, may
# hold no more than 256 open files (where lzrprf itself was started with a
# lower limit that no process may raise, that lower limit stands), may
# take no more memory than the shell that starts it holds plus 2 GiB
# ($PAGE_ROOM), and is told one thing: the address it lays out. It sees no
# other variable. Each limit is set before the next step, and a limit the
# system will not set starts nothing. Each is set twice, as the limit that
# counts now and as the limit that may not be raised again ("limit" below):
# which of the two a bare "ulimit" sets differs between the shells macOS can
# use as /bin/sh, and with one of them the program could have raised its
# limits again (found before release 17 went out; said here from then on).
my $PAGE_LIMITS = 'limit() { ulimit -S "$@" && ulimit -H "$@"; } && v=$(/bin/ps -o vsz= -p $$) && limit -t 30 && limit -f 0 && limit -c 0 && { limit -n 256 2>/dev/null || [ "$(ulimit -H -n)" -le 256 ] 2>/dev/null; } && limit -v $((v + ' . $PAGE_ROOM . ')) && exec /usr/bin/env -i "LZRPRF_CONTAINED=$LZRPRF_CONTAINED" ${LZRPRF_PICTURE:+"LZRPRF_PICTURE=$LZRPRF_PICTURE"} "$@"';

# What curl's container is allowed on top of it: one socket file, behind which
# lzrprf holds the connection to the site, and the list of certificate
# authorities. It has no network of its own. So whatever a site sends it, curl
# can reach no other place, read no file of yours, write nothing and start
# nothing.
my $FETCH = <<'END';
(allow network-outbound (literal (param "WAY")))
(allow file-read* (literal (param "ROOTS")))
END
# curl too gets at most 30 seconds of processor time, may write no byte into a
# file and no memory dump, may hold no more than 256 open files, and may take no
# more memory than the shell that starts it holds plus 2 GiB. When the system will not set one of these limits, curl
# is not started. It is started through "env -i", so it sees no variable at
# all, not one the shell that sets the limits made for itself either.
my $FETCH_LIMITS = 'limit() { ulimit -S "$@" && ulimit -H "$@"; } && v=$(/bin/ps -o vsz= -p $$) && limit -t 30 && limit -f 0 && limit -c 0 && { limit -n 256 2>/dev/null || [ "$(ulimit -H -n)" -le 256 ] 2>/dev/null; } && limit -v $((v + ' . $PAGE_ROOM . ')) && exec /usr/bin/env -i "$@"';

# The window's container. A window needs the system's services, so this one
# is written the other way round: what a program of yours may do, without
# the things below. No network of any kind. No writing of any file. No
# starting of any program but the one it is, in this same container. No asking the system to open an address, a file
# or another program for it. No command to another program. No writing of
# your settings. No system service but the ones named below, so no asking the system's downloader to fetch for it. No
# signal to any other process. No reading under /Users or on another disk, apart from your settings, your
# fonts, keyboard layouts and input methods. No file of the application is
# read: the icon in the Dock is drawn by the lines of "icon" in window_text.
# What it reads on descriptor 0 and writes on descriptor 1 is its talk with
# lzrprf.
# The system services it may look up by name are listed one by one: 41 that
# a window's process was seen to ask for on macOS build 26B123 (drawing,
# fonts, typing, the clipboard, the Dock and the menu bar, settings, the
